<p><strong>Mother refuses:1. I/V acyclovir 5mg/kg every 8hrs + neonatal acyclovir 20mg/ kg every 8 hoursMother refuses:1. I/V acyclovir 5mg/kg every 8hrs + neonatal acyclovir 20mg/ kg every 8 hours</strong><strong>Mother refuses:</strong>1. I/V acyclovir 5mg/kg every 8hrs + neonatal acyclovir 20mg/ kg every 8 hours<strong>Mother refuses:</strong>1. I/V acyclovir 5mg/kg every 8hrs + neonatal acyclovir 20mg/ kg every 8 hours</p>
<h3><strong>P</strong><strong>PROM:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Recurrent:</li><li>&lt;34 oral suppressive dose Acyclovir 400mg TDS till delivery + Erythromycin, steroid + SVD.</li><li>&gt;34 steroid + Erythromycin +IOL</li><li>P0 Herpes</li><li>before 34 weeks:</li></ul>
<h3>MDT discussion regarding MOD:</h3>
<ul><li>Expectant management</li><li>I/V acyclovir + Erythromycin + steroid + c/s according to MDT decision</li><li>Immediate birth – C/S.</li><li>After 34 weeks, steroid + C/S.</li></ul>
<p><strong>HIV + herpes:</strong> suppressive doses from 32 weeks.</p>
<p>Urinary retention =&gt; catheterize the patient</p>
<p>15% =&gt; unknown either P0 or recurrent for confirmation PCR is recommended but not in primary.</p>
<p>FSE, FBS, instrument can be applied in Recurrent.</p>', 6) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE content=VALUES(content), type=VALUES(type);
INSERT INTO topics (id, chapter_id, title, description, `order`, is_published, author, source) VALUES ('t-mm-09-006', 'chap-mm-09-01', '1.5. MALARIA IN PREGNANCY', 'Detailed medical reference and study guide on 1.5. MALARIA IN PREGNANCY.', 6, true, 'Dr. Farzana Muneer', 'Maternal Mind FCPS/MRCOG Textbook') ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE title=VALUES(title), description=VALUES(description), is_published=true;
INSERT INTO content_blocks (id, topic_id, type, content, `order`) VALUES ('cb-7ea76e7b', 't-mm-09-006', 'html', '<p><em><strong>Tertian Malaria –</strong></em> 48 hours’ cycle – P ovale, vivax, falciparum</p>
<p><em><strong>Quartan</strong></em><em><strong> Malaria –</strong></em> 72 hours’ cycle – P Malariae.</p>
<p><em><strong>Immunity lost</strong></em> within 2 years.</p>
<p><em><strong>Gold std</strong></em><strong> Test</strong><strong>:</strong> microscopic examination of thick &amp; thin film.</p>
<h3><strong>Incidences:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>75% falciparum, 0.5 – 1% Mortality</li><li>5.5% ovale 12.9% vivax, 2.1% Malariae</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Suspect malaria:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Fever + history of travel to endemic area or residence (&gt; 6m0).</li><li>Recent return 3 weeks – viral infections.</li><li>Chemoprophylaxis not 100% effective.</li><li>S/S + investigations – Rapid Testing + blood film (1 hour).</li><li><em><strong>Other Tests –</strong></em> CBC, BSL, U/E, blood C/S, urine dip, stool, CXR, USG.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Blood film shows:</strong></h3>
<ul><li><em>No evidence of Malaria.</em></li><li><em>Falciparum.</em></li><li><em>Non</em><em>-Falciparum</em><em>.</em></li></ul>
<h3><strong>1. No evidence of Malaria:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Repeat film – (after stopping prophylaxis).</li><li>3 negative films 12 – 24 hours.</li><li>apart exclude malaria so finish chemoprophylaxis.</li><li>Empirical Treatment – Severe malaria expected.</li><li>Stop prophylaxis before repeating (as it give negative report).</li></ul>
<h3><strong>2. Non – Falciparum:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Chloroquine – 600mg stat dose</li><li>300mg – 6 – 8 hours later.</li><li>300mg – Day 2.</li><li>300mg – Day 3.</li><li>To prevent relapse (vivax/ovale).</li><li>300mg – weekly chloroquine.</li><li>During pregnancy chloroquine resistant cases are treated by mefloquine.</li><li>To prevent relapse after delivery and testing for G6PD , primaquine is recommended. (avoid in pregnancy).</li></ul>
<h3><strong>3. Falciparum:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Mixed species or not characterized.</li><li>Admit.</li><li>Assess severity.</li><li>Complicated.</li><li>Uncomplicated.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Complicated:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Admit in HDU</li><li>&gt;2% parasitized</li><li>50% mortality.</li><li>Antimalarial immediately.</li><li>Fluid balance – CVP line.</li><li>Monitoring.</li><li>BSL – 1 hourly</li><li>4 hourly</li><li>HR.</li><li>Temp</li><li>RR.</li><li>SPo2.</li><li>JVP.</li><li>Repeat daily.</li><li>CBC</li><li>Parasite count.</li><li>LFT, RFT, clotting screen.</li><li>Treat for G negative bacteria.</li><li>Artesunate I/V 2.4mg/kg @ 0, 12, 24hours’ stat dose (drugs of choice any Gestation, then daily till patient is stable).</li><li>Quinine I/V 20mg/ kg in 5% D/W over 4 hours. Then 100mg/kg every 8 hours then oral when Pt can swallow.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Uncomplicated:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Admit in ward</li><li>&lt;2% parasitemia</li><li>Mortality 0.1%</li><li>Quinine 600mg 8 hourly + clindamycin 450mg 8 hourly (before 13 weeks drug of choice).</li><li>OR</li><li>Raiment 4-tab, B.D for 3days.</li><li>OR</li><li>Proguanil + Atovaquone (malarone) 4 doses daily – 3 days.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Some important points about Malaria:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Expert advice (especially recurrent &amp; severe).</li><li>Rapid detection negative in low parasitemia &amp; P – vivax.</li><li>Gold std microscopy thin/thick film.</li><li>Rapid detection followed by microscopy (Number of infected cells, species, stage).</li><li><strong>Premunition</strong>: Natural immunity acquired against malaria.</li><li>Low premunition – 90% with low premunition develop anemia.</li><li>UK population low premonition.</li></ul>
<h3><em><strong>Relapses –</strong></em> due to planta sequestration:</h3>
<ul><li>25 – 42 days’ average, relapse time</li><li>85 days after quinine.</li><li>98 days after Artesunate.</li><li>63 days with artemether, lumefantrine<strong> </strong></li><li><strong>Weekly films –</strong> reduces mortality due to recurrence.</li><li>treatment of recurrence =&gt; artesunate 84% success. Artesunate 100mg/ day for 7 days + clindamycin 45mg TDS – 7 days (WHO recommendation for relapse).</li><li>Hypoglycemia exacerbation by quinine =&gt; fetal distress.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Cinchonism, 7 days’ treatment:</strong></h3>
<ul><li><em>Headache.</em></li><li><em>Nausea.</em></li><li><em>Diarrhea. </em></li><li><em>Vomiting.</em></li><li><em>Blurred vision.</em></li><li><em>Auditory problems.</em></li><li>Hypoglycemia – due to hyper insulinemia.</li><li>commonest S/E of quinine.</li><li><strong>Infection control specialist</strong> =&gt; recurrent/ severe cases.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Treatment of complications:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Pulmonary edema – JVP aim of rt arterial pressure &lt;10cm H2O, (it has 50% mortality)</li><li>Anemia Hb &lt;8:</li><li>Pack cell + I/V frusemide 20mg.</li><li>Exchange transfusion.</li><li>Secondary bacterial infection when pt become hypotensive systolic B.P &lt;80mg</li><li>C/S =&gt; start ceftriaxone immediately due to risk of infection.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Thrombocytopenia:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Recovers 7 days in 90%.</li><li>&amp; 100% within 14 days</li><li>Of treatment.</li><li>Thrombocytopenia is risk</li><li>Of   blood loss in endemic</li><li>Areas but not PPH.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Fetal complications:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>IUGR, Pre – Term, fetal distress.</li><li>MDT – severe fetal compromise.</li><li>(ICU specialist, infectious disease specialist, obstetrician, neonatologist).</li><li>Aim is to prevent pre – term, IUD.</li></ul>
<h3><strong> VTE</strong><strong> Prophylaxis</strong><strong>:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Plt &lt;100, - withhold.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Peripartum</strong><strong> Malaria:</strong></h3>
<h3><em><strong>Pla</strong></em><em><strong>ce</strong></em><em><strong>nt</strong></em><em><strong>a</strong></em><em><strong>l sequestration</strong></em> =&gt; P Falciparum most common:</h3>
<ul><li>Is indication for following tests:</li><li>placental histopathology</li><li>Cord &amp; baby blood film   To detect</li><li>Every week for 28 weeks.</li><li>Congenital malaria 8-33%</li></ul>
<p>Positive</p>
<p>Inform womenregarding S/S ofmalaria in Baby.</p>
<p>=&gt; All Neonates of mother who had malaria inpregnancy had blood films @birth &amp; weeklyblood films for28 days.</p>
<h3><strong>Care of women after recovery from</strong><strong> </strong><strong>Malaria:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>Regular ANC, HB, Glucose, Growth scan, Plt.</li><li>FGR – 50% - Routine IUGR care.</li><li>Pre term labour =&gt; tocolysis + steroid + antimalarial.</li><li>Fetal distress =&gt; Antipyretics (PCM 1g every 4 – hours – max 4g/day) BSL monitoring</li><li>Still birth + pre – Term birth prevented by effective antimalarial treatment.</li><li>Miscarriages</li><li>IUD – Antimalarial can prevent IUD.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Complicated Malaria:</strong></h3>
<ul><li>1 of following:</li><li>Impaired consciousness.</li><li>Hypoglycemia &lt; 2.2 mmol.</li><li>ARDS, pulmonary edema.</li><li>Parasitemia &gt; 2%.</li><li>Severe anemia &lt; 8.</li><li>DIC.</li><li>Hemoglobinuria without GOPD deficiency.</li><li>Renal impairment:</li><li>Oliguria, 0.4ml/hr.</li><li>Creatinine &gt; 265 mmol.</li><li>Hyper lacticaemia:</li><li>Correlated with mortality.</li><li>Shock.</li><li>Acidosis &lt; 7.3.</li><li>Algal malaria – gm negative septicemia.</li><li>Jaundice is not a complicated feature.</li></ul>
<h3><strong>Malaria prevention ABCD</strong></h3>
<ul><li><em>Awareness of risk.</em></li><li><em>Bite prevention.</em></li><li><em>Chemo prophylaxis.</em></li><li><em>Diagnose + treat promptly.</em></li><li><strong>Awareness of risk:</strong></li><li>Avoid traveling to endemic areas.</li><li>Guidance on avoidance.</li><li>Specific risk associated with travel.</li><li>Educate on symptoms.</li><li><strong>Bite prevention:</strong></li><li>Repellents – 50% Deet.</li><li>Knock – down sprays.</li><li>Insecticide treated nets repeat every 6m0.</li><li>Clothing.</li><li>Room protection.</li><li><strong>Chemo prophylaxis </strong></li><li>not 100% not effective.</li><li><em><strong>Casual prophylaxis </strong></em></li><li>Continue for 7 days after leaving.</li><li>Against liver schizont.</li><li>Malarone (atovaquone + proguanil).</li><li>1 tab daily.</li><li>Avoid in 1st</li></ul>', 1) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE content=VALUES(content), type=VALUES(type);
